(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ophthalmic-pathophys-quiz"]={"title":"Ophthalmic Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clin Path I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the primary etiology of TRACTIONAL retinal detachment?","choices":["Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Severe malignant hypertension","Posterior vitreous detachment","Choroidal melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the neovascular membranes of proliferative disease are the classic traction source. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What proportion of severe blindness from macular degeneration does the wet form account for?","choices":["About ten percent","About ninety percent","About fifty percent","About twenty-five percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the wet form progresses rapidly, because leaking new vessels cause rapid central vision loss, so it accounts for about ninety percent of severe blindness. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What do the lids, cornea and conjunctiva have in common as the outermost ocular structures?","choices":["They are the only structures visible without an ophthalmoscope","They contain the highest density of nerve endings","They are the last to be affected in systemic disease","They are more susceptible to infection, trauma and environmental change"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — as the outer, easily visible structures, the lids, cornea and conjunctiva face the environment directly and are more susceptible to infection, trauma and environmental change. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is amblyopia?","choices":["Opacification of the crystalline lens","Reduced best-corrected acuity from abnormal visual development","Ocular misalignment arising from extraocular muscle imbalance or nerve palsy","Progressive optic neuropathy from raised pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — amblyopia is a visual deficit rather than a mechanical one: best-corrected acuity stays reduced because visual processing developed abnormally during the critical pediatric period. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of wet, neovascular macular degeneration?","choices":["Choroidal neovascularization breaching beneath the pigment epithelium","Extracellular lipofuscin debris accumulating beneath the retinal pigment epithelium","Fibrovascular membranes on the retinal surface exert traction","Ganglion cell axons undergo apoptosis from raised pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hypoxia and inflammation trigger choroidal neovascularization, and the new vessels breach beneath the pigment epithelium into the subretinal space, where they leak blood and serous fluid. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which change in the vitreous underlies rhegmatogenous retinal detachment?","choices":["It becomes vascularized, bleeding into the subretinal space","It thickens and opacifies, blocking light before it reaches the retina","It liquefies, so fluid tracks through a break into the subretinal space","It solidifies, pressing the retina more firmly against the pigment epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — once liquefied, vitreous fluid can pass through a full-thickness retinal tear into the subretinal space and peel the neurosensory retina off the pigment epithelium. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Above what cup-to-disc ratio is optic disc cupping progressive?","choices":["Greater than 1.0","Greater than 0.5","Greater than 0.2","Greater than 0.8"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — raised pressure compresses retinal ganglion cell axons, the cells die by apoptosis, and the cup widens to a cup-to-disc ratio above 0.5. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which findings does orbital cellulitis show that periorbital cellulitis lacks?","choices":["Erythema and edema of the lid alone, held forward by the orbital septum","Crusting of the lid margin with altered tear film","A yellowish nodule that does not cross the cornea","Proptosis, reduced eye movement and risk of optic nerve compression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bacteria breach the orbital septum into the soft tissue behind the globe, and the purulence there causes proptosis, reduced extraocular movement and optic nerve compression risk. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the classical corneal finding in herpes simplex epithelial keratitis?","choices":["A geographic ulcer with a hypopyon","A ring infiltrate in a contact lens wearer","A wedge of fibrovascular tissue growing across and over the corneal limbus","A dendritic ulcer with terminal bulbs on fluorescein staining"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the classical herpes simplex epithelial keratitis lesion is a branching dendritic corneal ulcer whose tips end in terminal bulbs on fluorescein stain. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the lipid layer of the tear film do?","choices":["It prevents evaporation","It provides the bulk volume of the tears","It anchors the tear film to the corneal epithelium","It carries immunoglobulin to the ocular surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — meibomian glands in the tarsal plates produce the lipid layer, which prevents evaporation; when they are obstructed, as in blepharitis, tears evaporate excessively. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of primary open-angle glaucoma?","choices":["Aqueous production by the ciliary body increases sharply","The canal of Schlemm is congenitally absent","An open angle, but trabecular meshwork resistance impairs outflow","The iridocorneal angle narrows anatomically and blocks aqueous outflow suddenly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the angle is open; the obstruction is microscopic resistance in the trabecular meshwork, downstream, so pressure rises gradually. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which two structures make up the fibrous outer tunic of the eye?","choices":["The iris and the lens","The sclera and the cornea","The choroid and the ciliary body","The retina and the retinal pigment epithelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the protective white coat and the clear refracting window. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How is episcleritis best characterized?","choices":["A granulomatous blockage of a meibomian gland","A self-limiting inflammatory blush, not vision-threatening and without severe pain","A vision-threatening emergency with severe deep boring pain and scleral melting","An infection of the corneal stroma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — inflammation of the superficial episclera is a self-limiting blush with no severe pain and no threat to vision, unlike the deep, emergency inflammation of scleritis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What causes an ipsilateral optic nerve lesion?","choices":["Temporal lobe surgery","Trauma, optic neuritis and ischemic optic neuropathy","Pituitary adenoma compression","Posterior cerebral artery occlusion in the occipital lobe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a lesion of the optic nerve before the chiasm follows trauma, optic neuritis or ischemic optic neuropathy, and causes total vision loss in that eye. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is a hypopyon?","choices":["Pus in the anterior chamber","Blood in the anterior chamber","Fluid beneath the neurosensory retina","Yellow debris beneath the retinal pigment epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a hypopyon is a collection of pus in the anterior chamber, the inflammatory layer seen in the front of a severely inflamed eye. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the conjunctival fornix?","choices":["A flexible junctional cul-de-sac that permits free rotation of the globe","The dense fibrous plate giving the eyelid its shape","The sphincteric muscle responsible for blinking","The circular junction where the conjunctiva meets the edge of the cornea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the fornix is the flexible junctional cul-de-sac where palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet, and its slack permits free rotation of the globe. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What makes the eye the only place in the body where live neural tissue can be seen directly?","choices":["The retina can be biopsied more safely than any other neural tissue","The cornea contains myelinated tracts that can be seen on slit lamp","The sclera is thin enough to transilluminate the whole globe","The optic nerve head is visible through the pupil noninvasively"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the eye is a nervous window: the only place in the body where live neural tissue and a cranial nerve, the optic nerve head, can be seen directly. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which description matches the optics of myopia?","choices":["The axial globe is too short, so the focal point falls behind the retina","The globe is normal but the cornea is irregularly curved","The globe is normal but the lens has lost elasticity","The axial globe is too long, so the focal point falls in front of the retina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an elongated globe overshoots, focusing short of the retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the hallmark of dry, atrophic macular degeneration?","choices":["Choroidal neovascularization breaching upward into the subretinal space","Neovascularization on the optic disc","Cotton-wool spots in the nerve fiber layer","Drusen: yellow extracellular debris beneath the pigment epithelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — drusen are discrete yellow extracellular deposits of lipofuscin and apolipoproteins beneath the pigment epithelium and Bruch membrane, the hallmark of the dry form. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A lesion of the optic tract produces which defect?","choices":["Contralateral homonymous hemianopsia","Bitemporal hemianopsia","Monocular blindness","Central scotoma with preserved peripheral vision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an optic tract lesion, from stroke, tumor or demyelination, causes contralateral homonymous hemianopsia, the loss of the opposite visual field. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Where must light be focused for an image to form?","choices":["On the lens itself, which then transmits the signal","On the retina, after refraction through the cornea and lens","On the choroid, after refraction through the cornea alone","On the optic disc, where the nerve exits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — image formation requires light refracted through the cornea and lens to be focused onto the retina, where rods and cones convert photons to signals. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which medication class is a cataract risk?","choices":["Chronic antihistamines","Chronic statins","Chronic corticosteroids","Chronic beta-blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chronic corticosteroid use is the medication cause of cataract, alongside aging, diabetes, ocular trauma, Down syndrome and ultraviolet light. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What can a hyperopic patient see well?","choices":["Objects up close","Neither near nor far without correction","Only objects directly in the central field","Objects far away"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — hyperopia is farsightedness, so distance vision is the preserved one. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is known about the pathogenesis of macular degeneration overall?","choices":["It is unknown","It is entirely genetic","It is entirely driven by ultraviolet exposure","It is a consequence of raised intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the pathogenesis of both dry and wet macular degeneration is unknown, even though drusen and choroidal neovascularization describe what happens in each. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How is the avascular cornea oxygenated?","choices":["By the choroidal circulation behind it","By direct contact with air and tears","By diffusion from the limbal blood vessels alone","By the aqueous humor circulating through the anterior chamber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the cornea is entirely avascular, so it is oxygenated through direct contact with air and tears rather than by any blood supply. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does a central chiasmal lesion spare nasal vision in both eyes?","choices":["Only the crossing nasal fibers are cut, and they carry the temporal fields","The lesion damages the temporal retinal fibers instead, and those carry nasal vision","The macula is spared by a dual blood supply","The lesion affects only one eye at a time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — only the nasal retinal fibers cross at the chiasm, and they carry the temporal fields; the temporal retinal fibers stay ipsilateral, so nasal vision survives. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which description fits the cones of the retina?","choices":["About 6 million, spread evenly across the whole retina","About 60 million, concentrated at the optic disc","About 6 million, concentrated in the fovea centralis within the macula","About 120 million, concentrated in the peripheral retina rather than the fovea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — about 6 million cones, serving color vision and sharp acuity, are concentrated in the fovea centralis within the macula. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which function of the eye does presbyopia impair?","choices":["Drainage of aqueous humor through the trabecular meshwork","Secretion of the lipid layer of the tear film","Accommodation for near objects","Light detection by the rods and cones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — presbyopia is loss of lens elasticity and ciliary muscle accommodation due to sclerosis, so the eye can no longer focus on near objects. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the symptoms of rhegmatogenous detachment?","choices":["Severe pain with halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil","Slow loss of central detail with progressive distortion of straight lines","Flashing lights, a shower of floaters, and a curtain falling across the field","Gradual painless bilateral blurring with glare at night"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the symptoms of a full-thickness retinal break with detachment are flashing lights (photopsia), a shower of floaters and a curtain falling across the field. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Macular degeneration is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in which group?","choices":["Children and adolescents","Adults of any age worldwide","Adults over seventy-five in the United States","Adults aged twenty to seventy-four in the United States"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — macular degeneration is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in United States adults over seventy-five, while diabetic retinopathy leads in younger adults. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
